'''''Gypsophila''''' ({{IPAc-en|dʒ|ɪ|p|ˈ|s|ɒ|f|ɪ|l|ə}})<ref>''Sunset Western Garden Book,'' 1995:606–607</ref><ref>[https://plants.usda.gov/java/ClassificationServlet?source=profile&symbol=GYPSO&display=63 ''Gypsophila''] at [https://plants.usda.gov/ USDA PLANTS Database]</ref> is a [[genus]] of [[flowering plant]]s in the carnation [[Family (taxonomy)|family]], [[Caryophyllaceae]]. They are [[native plant|native]] to Eurasia, Africa, Australia, and the Pacific Islands.<ref name=fna>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=114363 ''Gypsophila''.] Flora of North America.</ref> [[Turkey]] has a particularly high diversity of ''Gypsophila'' taxa, with about 35 [[endemism|endemic]] species.<ref>Korkmaz, M., et al. (2012). [http://www.cabdirect.org/abstracts/20123405442.html Habitat properties of some ''Gypsophila'' L. (Caryophyllaceae) taxa of Turkey.] ''Biyoloji Bilimleri Araştırma Dergisi (BİBAD)'' 5(2), 111-25.</ref> Some<ref>{{Cite web |last=גיבסנית |first=זר פרחים |title=גיבסנית |url=https://ella-flowers.co.il/product-category/%D7%92%D7%99%D7%91%D7%A1%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%AA |website=אלה פרחים}}</ref> ''Gypsophila'' are [[introduced species]] in other regions.<ref name=fna/>

The genus name is from the [[Greek language|Greek]] ''gypsos'' ("gypsum") and ''philios'' ("loving"), a reference to the [[gypsum]]-rich substrates on which some species grow.<ref name=fna/> Plants of the genus are known commonly as '''baby's-breath''', or bebe's breath,<ref name=fna/><ref name=itis>[https://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=20289 ''Gypsophila''.] Integrated Taxonomic Information System (ITIS)</ref> a name which also refers specifically to the well known [[ornamental plant|ornamental]] species ''[[Gypsophila paniculata]]''.

==Description==
''Gypsophila'' is one of the most heterogeneous and largest groups in the carnation tribe, [[Caryophylleae]]. The genus comprises approximately 150 species of [[annual plant|annual]] or [[Perennial plant|perennial]] herbaceous, creeping or cushion-forming plants, inhabiting primarily the mountainous [[steppe]]s in the north [[Temperate climate|temperate]] part of the [[Old World]] with a diversification hotspot in the [[Irano-Turanian Region|Irano-Turanian region]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Madhani |first1=Hossein |last2=Rabeler |first2=Richard |last3=Pirani |first3=Atefeh |last4=Oxelman |first4=Bengt |last5=Heubl |first5=Guenther |last6=Zarre |first6=Shahin |date=2018 |title=Untangling phylogenetic patterns and taxonomic confusion in tribe Caryophylleae (Caryophyllaceae) with special focus on generic boundaries |journal=Taxon |language=en |volume=67 |issue=1 |pages=83–112 |doi=10.12705/671.6 |bibcode=2018Taxon..67...83M |issn=1996-8175 |url=https://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/bitstream/2027.42/146908/1/tax6716.pdf |hdl=2027.42/146908 |doi-access=free}}</ref> These [[species]] also show major variation in [[inflorescence]] type ranging from many-flowered lax thyrses or [[panicle]]s (e.g., ''G. [[Gypsophila elegans|elegans]]'' M.Bieb., [[Gypsophila paniculata|''G. paniculata'']] L., [[Gypsophila pilosa|''G. pilosa'']] Huds.) to compact head-like cymes ( ''[[Gypsophila capitata|G. capitata]]'' M.Bieb., ''[[Gypsophila capituliflora|G. capituliflora]]'' Rupr., ''[[Gypsophila caricifolia|G. caricifolia]] Boiss''.), and few-(uni-)flowered [[raceme]]-like monochasia (e.g., ''[[Gypsophila bazorganica|G. bazorganica]]'' Rech.f., ''[[Gypsophila saponarioides|G. saponarioides]]'' Bornm. & Gauba). Each small flower has a cup-like calyx of white-edged green [[sepal]]s containing five [[petal]]s in shades of white or pink. The fruit is a rounded or oval capsule opening at valves. It contains several brown or black seeds which are often shaped like a [[kidney]] or a [[Gastropod shell|snail shell]].<ref name=fna/>

==Uses==
A few species are commercially cultivated for several uses, including [[floristry]], [[herbalism|herbal medicine]], and food. The baby's-breath most commonly used in [[Floral design|flower arrangements]] such as [[Flower bouquet|bouquets]] is the common gypsophila, ''G. paniculata''.<ref name=henry>Henry, M. [https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-77004-3_13#page-1 ''Gypsophila paniculata'' L. (baby's breath): ''in vitro'' culture and the production of gypsogenin saponins.] In: ''Medicinal and Aromatic Plants IV ''(pp. 187-206). Springer Berlin Heidelberg. 1993.</ref> ''G. elegans'' is also used as a cut flower.<ref>[http://staff.aub.edu.lb/~weblhort/Plants/Annuals/Annuals_plants/Gypsophila_elegans.htm ''Gypsophila elegans''.] {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131029185028/http://staff.aub.edu.lb/~weblhort/Plants/Annuals/Annuals_plants/Gypsophila_elegans.htm |date=2013-10-29 }} Landscape Horticulture. American University of Beirut.</ref>

The genus is a source of [[saponin]]s that can be used for many purposes, including the production of [[photographic film]] and [[Hemolysis|hemolytic]] laboratory [[reagent]]s. Their [[detergent]] qualities make them useful in [[soap]] and [[shampoo]].<ref name=henry/>

''G. rokejeka'' is used to make the [[dessert]] [[halva]].<ref name=henry/> Species are also ingredients in [[liqueur]], [[cheese]], and [[ice cream]], providing flavor, aroma, and crispness to foods.<ref name=kork>Korkmaz, M., et al. [http://eprints.ibu.edu.ba/600/1/issd2010_science_book_p552-p559.pdf Economic importance and using purposes of ''Gypsophila'' L. and ''Ankyropetalum'' Fenzl (Caryophyllaceae) of Türkiye.] {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304124912/http://eprints.ibu.edu.ba/600/1/issd2010_science_book_p552-p559.pdf |date=2016-03-04 }} In: 2nd International Symposium on Sustainable Development, June 8–9, 2010, Sarajevo.</ref>

Several species are [[hyperaccumulator]]s of [[boron]], and may be planted to absorb the element from polluted soils.<ref name=kork/>

The plant is also used in making a foam-like topping for a popular dessert called "[[kerebiç]]" in [[Mersin]] province of [[Turkey]].

==Ecology==
Some species are known as weeds, including the "aggressive ornamental" ''G. paniculata'', which [[invasive species|invades]] habitat and competes with native flora.<ref>[http://www.agf.gov.bc.ca/cropprot/babysbreath.htm Baby's Breath (''Gypsophila paniculata'').] Plant Health & Pest Management, Ministry of Agriculture, British Columbia.</ref>

The plant ''[[Gypsophila simonii|G. simonii]]'' ([[çöven]] in [[Turkish language|Turkish]]) is widely distributed in [[Turkey]], where it is a native species.

